---
import Base from '../../../layouts/Base.astro';
const title = "Premiere Pro won't import your SRT? How to fix it";
const jsonld = [{ '@context': 'https://schema.org', '@type': 'Article', headline: title, author: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'SubFixer' } }];
---
<Base title={`${title} | SubFixer`} description="A generic import error on an SRT file often comes from the file itself. Check the timestamps, the line endings and the real file type, then import again." path="/guides/premiere-pro-srt-import-error/" jsonld={jsonld} ogType="article">
  <article class="guide">
    <h1>{title}</h1>
    <p class="lede">Premiere Pro says "The importer reported a generic error" and does not say which line caused it. In most cases the fault is in the .srt file, not in Premiere. This guide lists the causes people report most often, shows how to check each one in a few minutes, and covers captions that stop halfway or land at the wrong time.</p>

    <h2>What the generic error means</h2>
    <p>Import a file with File &gt; Import (Ctrl+I on Windows, Cmd+I on Mac) and pick the .srt. In Premiere 2021 and later, the captions land on a captions track above the video tracks. When the import fails, the message is the same whatever the cause, so it does not point at a line. One beta build did show more detail, a line like "Parse Error: Invalid time stamp: 5.0 on line 2", which points at one bad time. Most failures come from the file itself, and the checks below cover the faults users report most.</p>

    <h2>Check the real format first</h2>
    <p>Open the file in a plain text editor such as Notepad or TextEdit. A real SRT file starts with a number, then a time line with an arrow in it, then the text. If the first line says <code>WEBVTT</code> or <code>[Script Info]</code>, the file is a VTT or ASS file with an .srt name. Renaming does not change what is inside. Convert it with the <a href="/vtt-to-srt/">VTT to SRT</a> tool or the <a href="/ass-to-srt/">ASS to SRT</a> tool instead.</p>
    <p>Then run the file through the <a href="/srt-validator/">SRT validator</a>. It lists each problem with the line it sits on. Work through the list from the top, and check it again after each fix, because a change at one line can make the next line look wrong.</p>

    <h2>The common causes</h2>
    <p>Work down this list. Each cause takes a minute to check in the validator or in a text editor.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Dots instead of commas in the times.</strong> SRT writes a time as 00:00:05,000, with a comma before the milliseconds. Files from some other tools write 00:00:05.000 instead, and the validator marks those lines as not in standard SRT form. Saving the file from SubFixer writes commas.</li>
      <li><strong>Line feed only line endings.</strong> Windows uses CRLF line endings, and some tools save plain line feeds (LF) instead. Saving the file from SubFixer writes CRLF, so one re-save covers this.</li>
      <li><strong>A blank line at the top.</strong> The first line of an SRT file should be the number 1. An empty line above it can be enough to make the import fail. Delete it and save the file again.</li>
      <li><strong>Letters in the wrong encoding.</strong> Accents and other alphabets can break an import when the file was saved in an old code page. One user report involved Ukrainian text. Save the file as UTF-8 with the <a href="/fix-subtitle-encoding/">fix subtitle encoding</a> tool, which saves in UTF-8.</li>
      <li><strong>A renamed VTT or ASS file.</strong> A file with a VTT or ASS inside and an .srt name fails for the reason in the first section. Convert it rather than renaming it again.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>When only part of the captions import</h2>
    <p>Users report that Premiere stops at the first bad line. The captions before that line arrive, and the rest do not. Find the last caption that came in, open the file at that point, and read the block after it. The fault is usually in that block: a dot in a time, a missing blank line between blocks, or a number out of order. Fix that block, save the file, and import it again.</p>

    <h2>When the captions start at the wrong time</h2>
    <p>If the captions import but start at 00:00 instead of the time the video's first line is spoken, the start time is the setting to change. The captions panel has Import Settings with a start time. Set it to match the video, import again, and compare the first caption and one near the end with the picture.</p>
    <p>If every caption is early or late by the same amount, the file itself is out of step with the video. Use the <a href="/shift-subtitle-timing/">shift subtitle timing</a> tool to move the whole file, then import the saved copy.</p>

    <h2>Check the timing once it imports</h2>
    <p>When the captions are in, play the first minute and check that the first caption appears as the speaker starts. Then jump to the last few minutes and check again. A file that lines up at both points usually lines up in between. If the gap grows steadily from the start to the end, the problem is more likely a frame-rate mismatch than a bad line, and the <a href="/guides/23976-vs-25-fps-subtitles/">23.976 vs 25 fps guide</a> explains how to tell and fix it.</p>
    <p>Captions can be in sync and still be too fast to read in busy scenes. The <a href="/subtitle-reading-speed/">subtitle reading speed</a> tool finds lines that ask for more than a viewer can read, rewraps long rows and gives fast lines more time where the gap allows.</p>

    <h2>Clear the media cache</h2>
    <p>Premiere keeps a media cache. Captions from an earlier failed import can stay in it, so a corrected file can seem to fail again. Users report that clearing the media cache fixed this for them. Search Premiere's help for "media cache" to find the clear option in your version, since menu names change between releases. Clear it, then import the fixed file.</p>

    <p>See also: <a href="/guides/srt-vtt-ass/">SRT, VTT and ASS: what is the difference?</a> covers which format each player expects. Picture-based subtitles need a different route, covered in <a href="/guides/sup-to-srt-guide/">the SUP to SRT guide</a>.</p>
  </article>
</Base>
